            <td data-label="No-MBA route">{item.noMba}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Weigh a full-time MBA specifically for this purpose against the conservative
    10%-of-total-education-budget heuristic before treating it as an automatic yes. If the complete
    family education budget is Rs 10 lakh, a strict starting benchmark is roughly Rs 1 lakh for the
    credential itself, with the remaining Rs 9 lakh staying available for living security, tools,
    certifications, and future upskilling. Spending materially more than that needs a specific,
    evidence-based reason — verified placement outcomes at a genuinely strong institute for this
    exact track, not brand prestige alone. A part-time or executive MBA, or no MBA at all paired with
    a real requirement-gathering portfolio, often reaches the same BA role for a fraction of the
    cost and without the two lost earning years.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      The MBA does not teach you how to extract a clear requirement from a confused stakeholder. It
      gets you into the room faster. If you already suspect the ambiguity-tolerance test above is a
      genuine strength, the no-MBA route is a legitimate, lower-risk way to prove that before
      committing years and money to the credential.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="domain-tracks">Domain specialization tracks: why "business analyst" is not one job</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part most generic BA content flattens into one job description. In practice, the
    domain you land in shapes your daily work, your pay ceiling, and how portable your skill is
    later, almost as much as the job title itself does.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
