  <h2 id="ai-risk">Will AI replace business analysts</h2>

  <p>
    This sits under almost every "is business analyst a good career" search, whether the person
    searching says it directly or not. The honest answer depends on which half of the job you
    actually spend your day doing.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What AI is already automating</div>
      <ul>
        {aiComparison.gold.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What still needs a human</div>
      <ul>
        {aiComparison.teal.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Meeting-summary and first-draft requirement tools are moving fast, and that risk sits almost
      entirely on the routine documentation side of the job — the exact work junior BAs used to be
      hired specifically to handle. It does not remove the need for the person who notices a
      stakeholder contradicted themselves twice in the same meeting, or who holds firm on a scope
      boundary a sponsor is quietly trying to expand for free.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Treat AI-drafted BRDs and meeting summaries as a first pass you correct, not a finished
    deliverable you forward. The BAs who get more valuable over the next few years are the ones who
    use the time AI saves on formatting to spend more time actually sitting with stakeholders and
    catching what the summary missed — not the ones racing to produce documents faster.
  </p>

  <h3>How to use this as leverage instead of just defending against it</h3>

  <div class="ai-stage-list">
