        <p>Feed a recorded stakeholder meeting into a summarisation tool for the first-draft BRD or process map, then spend your time checking it against what you actually heard in the room, not retyping it from scratch.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ai-stage">
      <span class="ai-stage__num">2</span>
      <div class="ai-stage__body">
        <div class="ai-stage__title">Next: redesign your week around the hours this frees up</div>
        <p>Spend the reclaimed documentation time on more stakeholder conversations and earlier scope-clarity work, since that is the layer AI cannot shortcut and it is exactly what shortens a project's most expensive phase — the mid-project scope fight.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ai-stage">
      <span class="ai-stage__num">3</span>
      <div class="ai-stage__body">
        <div class="ai-stage__title">Later: become known as the BA who catches what the AI summary missed</div>
        <p>Build a track record of spotting the requirement nobody said out loud or the contradiction buried in a long meeting transcript. That reputation, not documentation speed, is what becomes the scarce, well-paid skill as drafting tools keep getting faster.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ba-to-pm">The BA-to-product-manager progression path</h2>

  <p>
    A meaningful share of people who start in business analysis eventually move toward product
    management, and in Indian tech and fintech companies it is one of the more realistic transitions
    in either direction — if it is built deliberately, not hoped for after enough years pass. This
    bridge, more than the entry route you took, is usually what turns a BA title into a genuinely
    scalable <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> instead of a role that plateaus once the
    domain-learning curve flattens out.
  </p>

